` carry all three into the SpendRecord. The tests never exercise them: `tests/scripted_model.py` sets only `cost` in `response_metadata` and sets no `usage_metadata`, so every reply in every test records `model_id=None, input_tokens=None, output_tokens=None`. The unit tests assert only `cost_usd`, and the integration test asserts only the run and artwork ids. You could delete the model_id line, swap input and output, or make `_added` return 0 instead of None, and the suite would stay green. Core learning 'When a behaviour matters because something calls or reads it, test through that caller with a non-default value\u2026' applies here. Fix: let ScriptedModel attach a `model_name` and `usage_metadata` to a reply (and an unpriced or unusaged variant). Then assert on the recorded ReplyCost, and on the integration test's ledger row, that the tokens are summed across a multi-call reply, that the model id is the one the provider named, and that a reply whose calls report no usage stays None rather than 0.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/agent.py", "arrt/src/arrt/app.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_ask_agent.py", "arrt/tests/integration/test_ask_surface.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: one survivor of the old 'Ask writes no spend' wording, and a guard narrower than the claim it backs", "recommendation": "Read list: .claude/rules/learnings/core.md only (learnings-files --for-diff). Two rules bear, and each is filed in full as an observation. (1) 'When you retire, amend, descope or close anything, first grep the whole repo (no --include) for its nouns and OLD wording': .prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md:1548, the POST /api/ask/threads/{id}/replies row, still says 'none written; spend is the provider's'. No grep of the rest of the tree found another live survivor (searched for 'provider's own month', 'already includes', 'writes no row', 'no SpendRecord'). This is an ordinary finding, not rule-unenforced