 clears 'it ships / misleads into action': 'A write the store refuses is logged and the reply still ends', in the fragment body and observability-strategy.md:46. It holds only for ServiceError, so it is folded into the Goal 6 observation, whose code fix makes it true. The ask_tokens category rows, the 'however it ended' coverage and the Consumer note match the code. The ledger-based month figure now includes Ask, and the sidebar figure was always the provider's. Scope: none.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md", ".prawduct/changes/ask-spend.md"], "fid": "R-4"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 0, "note": 3}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "A ledger fault other than ServiceError leaves the reply's stream open: queue.put(None) is never reached", "recommendation": "Scope: class (every statement in `_run`'s finally that comes before `queue.put(None)`). `_record` catches only ServiceError. `store_write` translates only StorageError, and `durable._write` converts only `sqlite3.IntegrityError` to StorageError. So a `sqlite3.OperationalError` (disk full, read-only file, locked) or any other exception from `record_cost` escapes the `finally` in `_run`. That skips the reply's log line and `await queue.put(None)`, and `reply()` then waits forever in `while (line := await queue.get()) is not None`. The HTTP stream never closes, and the thread stays claimed until the client gives up. If the reply was cancelled, the new exception also replaces the CancelledError. `_record`'s docstring names this danger ('the stream's terminal event must reach the client either way'), but the guard does not cover it. Fix: put `queue.put(None)` in an inner `try/finally` so nothing before it can skip it, or catch Exception in `_record` with the same log.exception. Add a test whose record_cost raises a non-ServiceError (for example sqlite3.OperationalError) and asserts that the stream ends and the thread is freed.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/agent.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "The refused (budget_spent) and failed endings are claimed to record spend, but only answered, step_limit and cancelled are tested", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The change fragment and the `_run` comment say a reply that was refused or failed still records what it was billed. That holds because `_record` runs in the shared `finally`, but no test pins it for those two endings. Add one case: a ScriptedModel that bills a call and then raises (and one that raises the key-limit refusal), asserting a single ReplyCost equal to the calls billed before the fault.", "files": ["arrt/tests/unit/test_ask_agent.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Rolling back past this release breaks the month's spend read: the previous SpendCategory cannot parse an ask_tokens row", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `sqlite_discovery._spend` reads `SpendCategory(row['category'])` strictly, and the column has no CHECK. After this release writes `ask_tokens` rows, a prior binary's `list_spend_records(since=\u2026)` raises ValueError, which breaks `spend_in_month`, `art_discovery(action='spend')` and the budget figure. Run cost is not affected, because Ask rows carry no run. Either state in the release or rollback notes that a rollback must keep these rows from being read, or confirm that the deployment's rollback restores the pre-deploy backup (backlog #186 notes that today's at-start backup is taken after migrations).", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/discovery_records.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "record-lint change-fragment: closes: [337] is not a backlog id", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The relay from the manifest's record_lint reads: 'bad-closes: '337' is not a backlog item ID; write `owner/repo#N` for an issue or the markdown backlog's `PFX-XXXX`'. Write `closes: [brookstalley/arrt#337]` in .prawduct/changes/ask-spend.md.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/ask-spend.md"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Test evidence is stale: the suite has not been recorded against this tree", "recommendation": "Scope: none. `prawduct-hook test-status` reports 'stale: evidence predates session (2026-11-09T05:01:43Z < 2026-11-09T13:46:44Z)'. Run the curation suite (and the root suite) before the work lands. `verify-coverage` skipped (coverage_required is false).", "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The reply's end-of-stream depends on _record catching only ServiceError, but a real store failure arrives as a raw sqlite3 error", "recommendation": "Ask._run's finally now calls self._record(...) before the log line and before queue.put(None), and _record catches only ServiceError. store_write (services/store.py) turns only StorageError into ServiceError, and durable._write wraps only sqlite3.IntegrityError. A read-only catalogue file, 'database is locked' or a full disk raises sqlite3.OperationalError, which gets past the catch and out of the finally. When that happens the reply's log line is never written, the None sentinel is never queued, _lines blocks on queue.get() for good, the HTTP response never closes, the thread stays claimed (every later send gets a 409) until the client drops, and the task's exception is never retrieved. The test fakes exactly this case ('the catalogue file is read-only') as a ServiceError, so it passes for a failure the real store never raises in that form. Learning: 'When a comment justifies code by a constraint, or names what a guard excludes, check the constraint and what else the guard breaks'. The docstring promises 'the stream's terminal event must reach the client either way'. Scope: class. The fault is that every statement ahead of queue.put(None) in that finally can withhold the sentinel. The fix is a construction: put the sentinel (and the log line) in an inner try/finally so nothing above them can skip them, and make _record's catch match the failures that actually occur (Exception, logged, at this reply boundary). Pin it with a writer that raises a non-ServiceError such as sqlite3.OperationalError.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/agent.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_ask_agent.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Two accumulators sum the same per-call cost, one as float and one as Decimal", "recommendation": "_Tally now holds cost_usd (float, read by the stream_end metadata and the log line) and cost (Decimal, read by the ledger), both fed from the same response_metadata['cost'] in the same branch. One quantity has two homes, and the figure the curator sees under the reply can drift from the figure the ledger records. Keep only the Decimal and derive float(self.cost) where the stream metadata and the log line need a float. Scope: instance.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/agent.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The spend write runs synchronously on the event loop, against the module's own worker-thread rule for the service layer", "recommendation": "_run sends every other service call through asyncio.to_thread ('On a worker thread, as every tool call is: the service layer is synchronous'). _record calls record_spend directly on the loop thread, where it takes the store's single RLock. If a tool call on a worker thread holds that lock for a transaction, the whole server loop waits, including other replies and the Player's routes. The docstring's reason, that cancellation must not cancel the record, can be met off the loop with asyncio.shield(asyncio.to_thread(...)) in the finally. Alternatively, record why sub-millisecond writes make it safe to keep it on the loop. Scope: instance.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/agent.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "A reply's ledger row names only the last call's model while its cost sums every call", "recommendation": "_Tally.model_id is overwritten on each on_chat_model_end ('or self.model_id'), but cost and token counts are summed across calls. If OpenRouter routes the calls of one reply to different models (fallbacks, auto-routing), the SpendRecord attributes the whole reply to whichever model answered last. Either make ReplyCost.model_id's docstring say 'the last model to answer', or record null when the calls disagree. Scope: instance.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/agent.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "api-contract.md still says an Ask reply writes nothing and its spend is the provider's", "recommendation": "The `POST /api/ask/threads/{id}/replies` row in .prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md (around line 1548) ends with 'none written; spend is the provider's'. Every reply that is billed anything now writes an `ask_tokens` SpendRecord. data-model.md and observability-strategy.md were updated in this change; this third home of the same claim was not. A reader of the contract would conclude that Ask spend is missing from `art_discovery(action='spend')`. Update that cell to name the ask_tokens row. Scope: instance. Grep the artifacts for 'spend is the provider' and 'none written' when doing it.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/agent.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Ask._record catches only ServiceError, but the realistic ledger failures arrive as sqlite3.OperationalError, escape the reply's finally and leave the stream unterminated", "recommendation": "Measured by reading, not by running. DiscoveryService.record_spend raises ServiceError only for a negative cost (unreachable here: _record returns when cost <= 0), a bad reference (none is passed) and, via store_write, StorageError. durable.DurableStore._write translates only sqlite3.IntegrityError into StorageError (durable.py:692). So a read-only file, 'database is locked' or a full disk raises sqlite3.OperationalError straight through `except ServiceError`. The test fixture's message, 'the catalogue file is read-only', describes one of the failures the guard cannot catch. The raise happens inside _run's finally, before log.info and `await queue.put(None)`, so (1) the per-reply INFO line, the observability strategy's primary signal for Ask, is never written, (2) _lines stays in `await queue.get()` and never closes the HTTP stream, and (3) the thread's claim stays held, so the next send gets 409 until the client disconnects. (2) and (3) are inferred from the code path and were not observed. The only trace is asyncio's 'Task exception was never retrieved', which comes late or never. Fix: move the record so that a raise cannot skip the terminator (for example, put queue.put(None) in its own finally), and widen the catch to Exception at this boundary with a reasoned broad-except waiver, since this is the 'logged, reply still ends' boundary the records describe. Replace the fixture with one that raises what a real refusal raises (sqlite3.OperationalError), or one that goes through a real store opened read-only. That makes the observability-strategy.md and fragment claims true without editing them. Scope: instance.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/agent.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_ask_agent.py"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "api-contract.md's Ask replies row still says 'none written; spend is the provider's'", "recommendation": "`.prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md:1548`, the stores column of `POST /api/ask/threads/{id}/replies`, contradicts the change: each billed reply now writes an ask_tokens SpendRecord. A maintainer reading the contract for what this route writes is told 'nothing'. Fix the cell to name the ask_tokens row. Adding a `Retires:` phrase for the old wording to the fragment would let record_lint's retired-claim-survives check own any other survivor. Scope: instance.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/agent.py"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "A new SpendCategory member is a value the previous release cannot read, yet this rollback is still a checkout", "recommendation": "Downstream consumer: the prior release reading the same catalogue. sqlite_discovery._spend parses every row with `SpendCategory(row['category'])`, and list_spend_records without a run_id reads the whole table before filtering by window. So after a checkout-only rollback, the first stored ask_tokens row makes spend_in_month, and with it `art_discovery(action='spend')`, raise ValueError for every month. operational-spec.md's Rollback row says 'A rollback that crosses no migration is still the old two-step', and this change has no migration. This is inferred from reading the base code, which is the previous release's. The conversation_tokens precedent ('stays so those rows still read') shows the repo already knows an enum value is a stored-data contract. Fix by recording that a release adding a SpendCategory value is a restore-to-roll-back, as a migration is, or by making the read tolerate an unknown category. Scope: class (any StrEnum persisted by value and parsed strictly; search the persistence row parsers for `Enum(row[...])`).", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/discovery_records.py"], "oid": "O-13"}], "duration_seconds": 600, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/ask-spend`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": null, "base_reviewed": null, "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md", ".prawduct/changes/ask-spend.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [{"check": "change-fragment", "path": ".prawduct/changes/ask-spend.md", "line": null, "detail": "bad-closes: '337' is not a backlog item ID; write `owner/repo#N` for an issue or the markdown backlog's `PFX-XXXX`"}], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "lea